# Weekday and hour-of-day of the event, for alert_time rules.
#
# Why this exists
# ---------------
# Sagan's `alert_time` keyword fires only on a recurring day-and-hour window
# (src/aetas.c, Check_Time). Sigma has no recurring-time operator, so the window
# is expressed as a match on two derived fields, which this transform creates.
#
# What it reproduces
# ------------------
# Check_Time compares two things: the day of the week and the time as an HHMM
# integer. This transform exposes both from the event timestamp:
#
#   .sagan_event_weekday   0=Sunday .. 6=Saturday, matching SUNDAY..SATURDAY in
#                          the engine's day bitmask (src/sagan-defs.h)
#   .sagan_event_hhmm      hour and minute as one integer, e.g. 14:05 -> 1405,
#                          matching aetas.c's `current_time = atoi("HHMM")`
#
# Two honest divergences, both documented on the converted rules as
# D_ALERT_TIME_EVENT_CLOCK:
#
#   * Sagan evaluates the window against the wall clock at the moment it
#     processes the line (time(NULL)), not against the event's own timestamp.
#     This transform uses the event timestamp, which is what a SOC usually means
#     by "activity at a suspicious time" and coincides with Sagan's value under
#     near-real-time ingestion;
#   * aetas.c uses localtime(). This transform formats in UTC unless the Vector
#     instance is configured otherwise; align the timezone with the Sagan host's
#     for the window to mean the same thing.
#
# If the event carries no usable timestamp, the fields are left unset and an
# alert_time rule cannot fire on time, rather than firing on a guessed clock.

# A syslog or JSON source usually gives .timestamp as a timestamp already; a
# string one (as `vector vrl` passes) is parsed as RFC3339 first. Anything else
# leaves the fields unset rather than firing on a guessed clock.
ts = .timestamp
if is_string(ts) {
    ts = parse_timestamp(ts, "%+") ?? null
}

if is_timestamp(ts) {
    .sagan_event_weekday = to_int!(format_timestamp!(ts, "%w"))
    .sagan_event_hhmm = to_int!(format_timestamp!(ts, "%H%M"))
}
